#!/bin/sh
# pre-commit: `npm run scan:secrets` then `npm run typecheck`.
#
# Every command a hook runs is a command CI runs too. A hook is never the only
# place a rule lives, so `--no-verify` defers feedback to CI rather than skipping
# a check — nothing here can be bypassed permanently.
#
# Why these two at this stage: a leaked credential is the only irreversible
# mistake in this repository (the `client_secret` travels in a query string, and
# history already needed one sanitisation pass), so the secret scan belongs in
# the cheapest, most frequent gate. Typecheck joins it because it is the fastest
# way to catch a change that cannot possibly be correct.
#
# Known limitation, stated rather than worked around: both commands read the
# **working tree**, not the staged snapshot. With unstaged changes present they
# validate something other than what is being committed. The obvious fix
# (`git stash --keep-index`) is deliberately not implemented: an interrupted hook
# can then lose work, and a documented limitation beats an unexplainable one.
# `scan:secrets` enumerates `git ls-files`, so newly staged files *are* included,
# but their contents still come from disk.
set -eu

npm run scan:secrets
npm run typecheck
